Mr. McNabb stops here.

>Among the Roses
You are among rows of roses. The ground is soft, and your footsteps leave a
rather bad impression as many poor seedlings are trampled underfoot. A safer
place to admire the flowers lies to the north. A window to the south allows a
view into the house.
There is no way into the house from here.
McNabb gives you a sideways glance. "What kept you?" he asks.
McNabb grabs your arm and leads you to a spot deep within the garden and near
the house. You might never have found this place alone. He points at the ground,
where you see two holes in the soft earth.

>Among the Roses
You are among rows of roses. The ground is soft, and your footsteps leave a
rather bad impression as many poor seedlings are trampled underfoot. A safer
place to admire the flowers lies to the north. A window to the south allows a
view into the house.
There is no way into the house from here.
There are holes in the soft dirt near your feet.

>There are two holes here, each about two inches by four inches. They are at
least three inches deep and the soil is compacted around them.

>You are making quite a mess, but you do run across some tiny pieces of a hard,
shiny substance, which drop from your fingers and back onto the ground.

>Although everything is coming up roses, you haven't found anything unusual
except for a few pieces of a hard substance which fall back to the ground.

>Ouch! You cut your finger on a sharp edge as you dig. You search carefully in
the dirt, now that you are sure something is there, and pull up a piece of
porcelain, covered with dirt and dried mud.

>Aside from dirt, organic fertilizer, and small crawly creatures, you turn up
nothing but roses.

>The piece of porcelain is filthy, coated with dried mud. You can barely make out
some design underneath the dirt.

>As you wipe off the piece of porcelain, you notice that it is a fragment of some
very beautiful piece, handsomely painted.

>The fragment is beautifully hand-painted.

>It's covered with dirt and mud. You realize that there would be no good
fingerprints on it.

>Sergeant Duffy walks up as quietly as a mouse. He takes the fragment from you.
"I'll return soon with the results," he says, and leaves as silently as he
entered.

>Time passes...
Sergeant Duffy returns with the fragment. For a moment you muse on his almost
magical entrances. "The fragment did contain LoBlo. Here is the full report." He
hands you a slip of paper, and departs.
It is now 12:14 pm.

